  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 250 PM CDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nVery hot temperatures continue across much of western and\ncentral North Dakota this Saturday afternoon. Hettinger had\nalready reached 104 degrees as of noon MDT. Many other locations\nsouth and west of the Missouri River are also around the 100 \ndegree mark early this afternoon. In Bismarck, our morning fog \nheld temperatures down a bit through mid morning, but we are \ncatching up quick with an early afternoon reading in the mid \n90s. The Heat Advisory started at 1 PM CDT and no changes here \nwith temperatures and/or heat indices in the 100-105 range for \nthe Advisory area. \n\nThe risk for thunderstorms remains low tonight, especially over\nnorthern and eastern portions of the forecast area. The better\nforcing for ascent will remain over southeast Montana into\nnorthwest South Dakota, yet a stronger storm or two can not be\nruled out in the far southwest, producing a marginally severe \nwind gust. \n\nSunday will be a repeat of today temperatures wise, with \nforecast highs once again expected to be over 100 degrees over \nmuch of the southwest and south central. Only slightly cooler \ntemperatures in the 90s are anticipated along the International \nBorder. The very hot temperatures should keep thunderstorm \nchances to a minimum. If anything, perhaps an isolated \nshower/storm brushing the International Border Sunday morning, \nand possibly some dissipating convection moving into the west \nlater Sunday night. Most of the day will be sunny and hot. The \nthreat for severe storms Sunday is minimal. \n\nThe very hot and dry conditions Sunday, combined with a dry cold\nfront passage, may create near critical fire weather concerns\nSunday afternoon and early evening. Afternoon relative \nhumidities will drop to 12 to 18 percent across much of western \nNorth Dakota. Northwest winds will increase behind the dry cold \nfront to 15 to 20 mph with gust to 30 mph. Due to the recent dry\nconditions, grasses have begun to cure and near critical fire \nweather conditions may be reached.\n\nOn Monday, the upper level ridge over the Northern Plains\nflattens a little bringing cooler temperatures northeast, but\nwith very hot temperatures still possible in the southwest and\nfar south central. Will hold off on any extensions of the Heat \nAdvisory, but this is something that may be needed as we get \ncloser to Monday. As was mentioned above, daytime thunderstorm \nactivity will be suppressed due to the hot temperatures. A \nmorning or overnight isolated thunderstorm can not be ruled out \nthough.\n\nThe remainder of the work week will continue to bring hot\ntemperatures, mainly in the 90s, but with some 100 degree \nreadings still possible in the southwest. Overall though, not \nquite as hot as this weekend. Daily low chances (20-30%) \nthunderstorm chances will remain through the week but with a low\npredictability in when and where they may occur. There does \nlook to be a little better chance of precipitation in the\nWednesday/Thursday timeframe with another impulse that briefly\nflattens the mean upper level ridge.",
